# Line endings. The kit targets WSL2, so a checkout from Windows git with
# core.autocrlf=true is the realistic way a CR gets into a script. A CR after
# a shebang or inside a heredoc breaks check.sh, and a CR in fixtures breaks
# the graders' anchored greps. Store and check out LF everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Executed or graded content: force LF even if git's text sniffing says
# otherwise. (21 soc check.sh files embed one literal CR on purpose, in
# `tr -d '<CR>'`, to strip Windows line endings from learner answers; that
# makes git sniff them as binary. Forcing text only normalises CRLF, so the
# lone CR is left alone.)
*.sh text eol=lf
bin/lab text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.psm1 text eol=lf

# Real binaries: never convert.
*.pcap binary
*.jpg binary
*.docm binary
*.pdf binary
*.exe binary
